  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "NBC digests CWB and the Laurentian retail/SME book on schedule: revenue grows ~6% for two years (organic loan growth ~5%, LBC adds ~1pp) and then slides toward nominal GDP, Capital Markets gives back part of its exceptional 2026 trading run, and PCL settles at ~35bp. Adjusted ROE holds ~16.5-17%, payout plus buybacks run ~70% of earnings, and the bank compounds as a high-return, Quebec-anchored franchise with a national footprint, not a re-rating story.",
    "bull": "The CWB deal proves to be a genuine pan-Canadian platform: Western Canada commercial cross-sell and the Calgary/Edmonton capital-markets build add revenue synergies on top of cost synergies, the fee-heavy Wealth and Capital Markets franchises keep outgrowing the balance sheet, credit stays benign, and management beats its 17%+ ROE target. Revenue compounds 7-9% for three years and margins widen ~1.5pp.",
    "bear": "A tariff-driven Canadian slowdown hits Quebec housing and the commercial book acquired with CWB: PCL roughly doubles to ~60bp for two years, trading revenue mean-reverts hard from 2026 highs, and two concurrent integrations bring client attrition and cost overruns. Revenue stalls, margins fall ~5pp before partly recovering, and ROE sits near 13-14% - still profitable and well-capitalised (CET1 13.5%), so this is an earnings bear, not a solvency bear."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "A bank is valued on the equity cash it can distribute after holding the capital its growth needs, discounted at the cost of equity. Deposits are funding, not debt, so there is no net-debt bridge. The question is whether NBC's premium P/B (~2.4x book, ~16x TTM adjusted EPS) is earned by its best-in-class ROE.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Capital Markets revenue (~26% of the mix, Q3 FY2026 +34% YoY) is at a cyclical high; the base already trims it, the bear removes it",
      "Quebec housing concentration plus the CWB commercial book in a tariff-hit economy: PCL is the largest swing factor in the flow",
      "CET1 run-rate: a regulator- or market-driven move to 13.5-14% raises retention and cuts FCFE ~3-5%",
      "Cost of equity: +/-50bp moves the base value roughly -/+10%",
      "Laurentian retail/SME close (targeted Nov 2026) and CWB AIRB model transition deferred to FY2027 (RWA relief timing)"
    ]
  },
